-- title:
--   Blocks, towns, majority types, and the favored graph
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with a configuration $\Gamma$ and a minimum apex angle $\vartheta$. The module introduces the renormalised combinatorial objects on which the chaining argument runs.
--
--   **The aperture-shrinking constant.** The threshold at which passing from apex angle $\vartheta/2$ to apex angle $\vartheta$ absorbs a whole cube at each end is taken to be
--
--   $$\delta(d,\vartheta) \;=\; \frac{\sqrt d + 1}{\sin(\vartheta/2)}.$$
--
--   The file comment records that the source's value $3\sqrt d/(2\sin\vartheta)$ is too small: at the threshold distance the available gap can be as small as $\ell\sqrt d\,\sin(\vartheta/2)/(2\sin\vartheta)$, always less than the $\ell\sqrt d/2$ the estimate requires.
--
--   **Blocks and towns.** A **block** is the set of lattice points inside a closed cube, $Q_\ell(x) = \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \bar A_\ell(x)$. A town of scale $(h,\ell)$ is **$\vartheta$-sparsely populated** when $\delta(d,\vartheta) < h/\ell$. The town is identified with the integer lattice by $x \mapsto Q_\ell(hx)$, and the blocks whose index lies within $R$ of a point $z$ form the **town ball**, the image of $\{p \in \mathbb{Z}^d : \|p - z\| < R\}$ under that identification.
--
--   **Majority types.** For a block $Q$ and a double cone $V$, the **fibre** of $\Gamma$ over $V$ inside $Q$ is $\{x \in Q : \Gamma(x) = V \text{ as sets}\}$, and $V$ is **favored by majority in $Q$** when its fibre is of maximal size:
--
--   $$\forall W:\quad \#\big\{x \in Q : \Gamma(x) = W\big\} \;\le\; \#\big\{x \in Q : \Gamma(x) = V\big\}.$$
--
--   **The favored graph.** There is a directed edge from block $Q$ to block $P$ when some cone $V$ favored by majority in $Q$ contains every point of $P$ when based at every point of $Q$, i.e. $y - x \in V$ for all $x \in Q$, $y \in P$. Adjacency in the favored graph is the symmetrised version together with an explicit clause $Q \ne P$.
--
--   Blocks are the vertices at which the whole argument is renormalised: a block is treated as a single site carrying one cone type, its majority type, so a configuration on the lattice becomes a configuration on the town, and the chaining can be run at the coarser scale.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Fibre sizes are `Set.encard` values in $\mathbb{N}^{\infty}$, so a majority type is one whose fibre has maximal extended cardinality; on infinite blocks the condition is therefore weak. Cone types are compared as *carriers*, following the convention that a type is the double cone as a set. The explicit clause $Q \ne P$ in the adjacency is needed because the edge condition holds vacuously between two empty blocks, which would make the relation reflexive there and prevent it from being a simple graph's adjacency; on nonempty blocks it changes nothing, since a self-edge would place $0$ in a cone.
